00:01:31.260 --> 00:01:34.950
Let's start with a
very quick recap.

00:01:34.950 --> 00:01:42.010
So pod is the smallest unit
of deployment in Kubernetes.

00:01:42.010 --> 00:01:45.610
A pod consist of one
or more containers that

00:01:45.610 --> 00:01:49.240
will be scheduled atomically.

00:01:49.240 --> 00:01:53.410
The containers in a pod can
share an IP address, local host

00:01:53.410 --> 00:01:56.110
networking, and
inter-process communication.

00:01:59.910 --> 00:02:03.090
Deployment is a
Kubernetes abstraction,

00:02:03.090 --> 00:02:06.450
which helps you to
manage your application.

00:02:06.450 --> 00:02:10.530
It offers straightforward
deployment, advanced updates,

00:02:10.530 --> 00:02:13.740
including rolling update,
and self-healing mechanism.

00:02:16.420 --> 00:02:19.820
Node is a machine
where containers run.

00:02:19.820 --> 00:02:22.670
When using Kubernetes, you
shouldn't care about it.

00:02:22.670 --> 00:02:25.160
It's Kubernetes, it manages
your containers rather than

00:02:25.160 --> 00:02:26.170
machines.

00:02:26.170 --> 00:02:30.160
This can be either a virtual
machine in a cloud environment,

00:02:30.160 --> 00:02:34.430
or physical machine
when you run on-prem.

00:02:34.430 --> 00:02:37.580
And kubectl is a common
light client tool

00:02:37.580 --> 00:02:40.790
which allows you to perform
all operation on Kubernetes

00:02:40.790 --> 00:02:44.950
cluster, like
managing your pods.

00:02:44.950 --> 00:02:48.140
So let's get back to our team.

00:02:48.140 --> 00:02:49.600
The code is written.

00:02:49.600 --> 00:02:52.430
The initial version of
the project is ready.

00:02:52.430 --> 00:02:55.120
It's high time to
introduce it to the world.

00:02:55.120 --> 00:02:57.960
So let's go.

00:02:57.960 --> 00:03:01.840
To render application, we
will use kubectl run command.

00:03:01.840 --> 00:03:03.820
The only mandatory
parameters are

00:03:03.820 --> 00:03:09.430
the deployment name, which
is bar-app in our case,

00:03:09.430 --> 00:03:11.770
and container image to run.

00:03:11.770 --> 00:03:14.420
We also specify optional
parameters here.

00:03:14.420 --> 00:03:18.580
CPU requests to one core,
which will be guaranteed amount

00:03:18.580 --> 00:03:20.710
of resources for the pod.

00:03:20.710 --> 00:03:24.110
This will also help scheduler
to make optimal decision,

00:03:24.110 --> 00:03:28.030
while choosing a node
for our pod to run.

00:03:28.030 --> 00:03:31.180
This is required by
Horizontal Pod Autoscaler,

00:03:31.180 --> 00:03:33.860
and I'll explain
the reason later.

00:03:33.860 --> 00:03:38.020
Expose flock will end up with
the service being available

00:03:38.020 --> 00:03:43.300
externally via
public IP address.

00:03:43.300 --> 00:03:46.090
In GKE, this means that
the Google Cloud load

00:03:46.090 --> 00:03:49.090
balancer is created underneath.

00:03:49.090 --> 00:03:53.550
Port is the default HTTP port.

00:03:53.550 --> 00:03:56.580
OK so the deployment
was successfully created

00:03:56.580 --> 00:04:00.330
and exposed externally,
which means that our app will

00:04:00.330 --> 00:04:01.555
be available in the internet.

00:04:05.680 --> 00:04:07.660
How can we access
our application?

00:04:07.660 --> 00:04:11.500
We need to know details
about the created service.

00:04:11.500 --> 00:04:14.150
This can be done with
kubectl get service command.

00:04:17.200 --> 00:04:20.230
We can see some information
about service bar-app.

00:04:20.230 --> 00:04:24.550
The most interesting for
us is external IP address.

00:04:24.550 --> 00:04:25.460
We can see it here.

00:04:28.010 --> 00:04:30.080
The application is
publicly available.

00:04:30.080 --> 00:04:31.190
We have some users.

00:04:31.190 --> 00:04:32.290
And they are happy.

00:04:32.290 --> 00:04:34.460
It's easy.

00:04:34.460 --> 00:04:36.420
We have more and more users.

00:04:36.420 --> 00:04:37.460
The team is happy.

00:04:37.460 --> 00:04:38.960
The management is happy.

00:04:38.960 --> 00:04:40.590
Everyone is happy.

00:04:40.590 --> 00:04:46.360
But one day something
bad has happened.

00:04:46.360 --> 00:04:49.870
Users started claiming that
our service doesn't work.

00:04:49.870 --> 00:04:53.760
The team has to investigate the
problem as soon as possible.

00:04:53.760 --> 00:04:55.840
OK, so let's do it.

00:04:55.840 --> 00:04:58.600
The first obvious idea
which comes to our mind

00:04:58.600 --> 00:05:00.580
is to check application logs.

00:05:00.580 --> 00:05:04.420
We can do it with
kubectl logs command.

00:05:04.420 --> 00:05:07.090
The command allows you
to get most recent logs

00:05:07.090 --> 00:05:12.450
from containers
stderr and stdout.

00:05:12.450 --> 00:05:16.610
In our case, we can see that
not every single HTTP request

00:05:16.610 --> 00:05:17.870
succeeded.

00:05:17.870 --> 00:05:21.150
The application was not
able to handle some of them.

00:05:21.150 --> 00:05:27.120
So the hypothesis is that we
have some performance issues.

00:05:27.120 --> 00:05:29.790
To confirm that, we will
check resource usage

00:05:29.790 --> 00:05:31.290
of the application.

00:05:31.290 --> 00:05:35.790
We can use kubectl top command,
which can display pod and node

00:05:35.790 --> 00:05:36.810
level resources.

00:05:40.880 --> 00:05:44.010
Indeed, the CPU usage is high.

00:05:44.010 --> 00:05:49.350
As you may remember, we
requested 1,000 millicores

00:05:49.350 --> 00:05:51.360
to be available for the pod.

00:05:51.360 --> 00:05:54.950
So this means that
CPU is saturated.

00:05:54.950 --> 00:05:58.580
In such situation, Kubernetes
will try to the pod.

00:05:58.580 --> 00:06:01.140
The diagnosis is clear.

00:06:01.140 --> 00:06:03.060
We just need more resources.

00:06:03.060 --> 00:06:06.410
We can either request
more resources for the pod

00:06:06.410 --> 00:06:09.900
or create yet another
instance of the application.

00:06:09.900 --> 00:06:12.120
The second option
is better for us

00:06:12.120 --> 00:06:15.300
since this will also
provide more reliability.

00:06:15.300 --> 00:06:18.520
For example, when a node where
one instance of application

00:06:18.520 --> 00:06:21.000
is running goes
down, we'll still

00:06:21.000 --> 00:06:23.930
have another instance
running on healthy nodes.

00:06:27.080 --> 00:06:30.290
In Kubernetes, we can
easily add new instances

00:06:30.290 --> 00:06:34.070
of the application by using
kubectl scale command.

00:06:34.070 --> 00:06:37.490
We need to specify the
deployment name and target

00:06:37.490 --> 00:06:39.470
number of replicas.

00:06:39.470 --> 00:06:41.690
The instance will be
automatically detected

00:06:41.690 --> 00:06:43.190
by the load balancer.

00:06:43.190 --> 00:06:47.310
And the traffic
will be sent there.

00:06:47.310 --> 00:06:50.160
We may want to check the
status of our application.

00:06:50.160 --> 00:06:52.380
You can see that we
have two instances

00:06:52.380 --> 00:06:54.280
and both are available.

00:06:54.280 --> 00:06:55.560
So, problem solved.

00:06:58.320 --> 00:07:01.170
The team is happy.

00:07:01.170 --> 00:07:03.210
They were able to
resolve the problem.

00:07:03.210 --> 00:07:05.670
But Susan, the lead
engineer, is wondering

00:07:05.670 --> 00:07:08.280
whether they need to
scale application manually

00:07:08.280 --> 00:07:10.750
all the time.

00:07:10.750 --> 00:07:12.230
So, of course not.

00:07:12.230 --> 00:07:15.390
To decrease personal
overhead, Kubernetes

00:07:15.390 --> 00:07:19.470
offer autoscaling features,
which basically are designed

00:07:19.470 --> 00:07:23.740
to handle traffic fluctuations.

00:07:23.740 --> 00:07:27.260
One of them is Horizonal
Pod Autoscaler,

00:07:27.260 --> 00:07:31.870
which is an answer for the
problem we just resolved.

00:07:31.870 --> 00:07:34.000
Let me explain how does it work.

00:07:37.080 --> 00:07:40.680
So the idea behind,
it is fairly simple.

00:07:40.680 --> 00:07:45.660
There is a Kubernetes controller
which observes application.

00:07:45.660 --> 00:07:50.430
And when it notices more
users of the application,

00:07:50.430 --> 00:07:54.540
it will add more instances in
order to handle traffic spike.

00:07:54.540 --> 00:07:56.850
Of course, it also
supports scaling down,

00:07:56.850 --> 00:07:59.730
where less users
mean just less pods.

00:07:59.730 --> 00:08:03.930
Scaling down helps us to
save compute resources.

00:08:03.930 --> 00:08:06.990
Horizontally Pod Autoscaler,
are also known HPA,

00:08:06.990 --> 00:08:11.220
has other capabilities.

00:08:11.220 --> 00:08:13.460
It works in the way
that it periodically

00:08:13.460 --> 00:08:15.150
adjusts the number
of application

00:08:15.150 --> 00:08:17.880
replicas up in demand.

00:08:17.880 --> 00:08:21.690
It ensures that average CPU
utilization of the application

00:08:21.690 --> 00:08:24.630
pods meets the target value.

00:08:24.630 --> 00:08:29.730
HPA works within defined
minimum and maximum boundaries.

00:08:29.730 --> 00:08:33.299
And it supports a number
of Kubernetes obstructions,

00:08:33.299 --> 00:08:35.429
which have ability
to be resized,

00:08:35.429 --> 00:08:39.559
including replica
set and deployment.

00:08:39.559 --> 00:08:48.140
OK, how to use Horizontal
Pod Autoscaler.

00:08:51.620 --> 00:08:53.960
We can set up scaling
our deployment

00:08:53.960 --> 00:08:57.830
by using kubectl
autoscale command.

00:08:57.830 --> 00:09:00.410
We specified the
deployment to be scaled,

00:09:00.410 --> 00:09:05.030
we is in our case, bar-app
minimum and maximum

00:09:05.030 --> 00:09:08.650
number of replicas, which
is between 1 and 10,

00:09:08.650 --> 00:09:13.620
and the target CPU
utilization to 70%.

00:09:13.620 --> 00:09:18.210
As you may remember, we said
CPU request to 1,000 millicores.

00:09:18.210 --> 00:09:22.470
HPA, we try to keep average CPU
utilization of our deployment

00:09:22.470 --> 00:09:24.630
around 70%.

00:09:24.630 --> 00:09:28.020
This means that when
instances of our application

00:09:28.020 --> 00:09:33.210
use more than 700 millicores
of CPU, by average,

00:09:33.210 --> 00:09:36.990
the controller will rescale
the size of the deployment up

00:09:36.990 --> 00:09:39.840
in the same way that
we did manually.

00:09:39.840 --> 00:09:42.060
It will prevent
from the situation

00:09:42.060 --> 00:09:46.130
when a pod will saturate
all available CPU.

00:09:49.310 --> 00:09:54.890
And here is some rumors from
the Kubernetes community.

00:09:54.890 --> 00:09:57.680
The community works
currently on introducing

00:09:57.680 --> 00:09:59.800
two more autoscaling features.

00:09:59.800 --> 00:10:02.630
The first feature is
ability to scale application

00:10:02.630 --> 00:10:05.430
based on signals
from inside of it,

00:10:05.430 --> 00:10:11.430
like a number of HTTP requests
or average requests latency.

00:10:11.430 --> 00:10:14.900
This is important when research
consumption of deployment

00:10:14.900 --> 00:10:20.100
doesn't translate one to one
to the necessity of scaling.

00:10:20.100 --> 00:10:23.750
Another features that Kubernetes
community is working on

00:10:23.750 --> 00:10:25.950
is Vertical Pod
Autoscaler, which

00:10:25.950 --> 00:10:28.260
instead of tuning
number of replicas,

00:10:28.260 --> 00:10:31.800
we'll be able to adjust
research the requirements

00:10:31.800 --> 00:10:33.850
of the application.

00:10:33.850 --> 00:10:35.660
This is important
when application

00:10:35.660 --> 00:10:38.950
doesn't scale horizontally.

00:10:38.950 --> 00:10:43.430
Once the feature are available
in [INAUDIBLE] of Kubernetes,

00:10:43.430 --> 00:10:45.200
they will be, of course,
available in GKE.

00:10:47.890 --> 00:10:50.800
OK so we solved the problem.

00:10:50.800 --> 00:10:56.260
And we automatized the
process so we can feel safe.

00:10:56.260 --> 00:10:57.750
Everyone is happy again.

00:10:57.750 --> 00:11:01.190
But again, something
bad has happened.

00:11:01.190 --> 00:11:02.480
Let's open the panic room.

00:11:05.330 --> 00:11:07.750
Our application was
supposed to be autoscaled.

00:11:07.750 --> 00:11:10.840
Let's check its status.

00:11:10.840 --> 00:11:14.400
We can see, indeed, it was
scaled up to 10 instances,

00:11:14.400 --> 00:11:18.830
but only six of
them are available.

00:11:18.830 --> 00:11:21.350
We can learn more
about the application

00:11:21.350 --> 00:11:25.420
by using kubectl
describe command.

00:11:25.420 --> 00:11:27.460
The output of the
command is usually

00:11:27.460 --> 00:11:30.580
a huge blob of various
kinds of information.

00:11:30.580 --> 00:11:32.920
You can see only
a piece of them.

00:11:32.920 --> 00:11:36.205
And what is most interesting
for us is the events section.

00:11:38.950 --> 00:11:46.460
There we can see that our
pod failed to be scheduled.

00:11:46.460 --> 00:11:49.400
The message says
insufficient CPU,

00:11:49.400 --> 00:11:51.710
which means that
on any node there

00:11:51.710 --> 00:11:54.680
is not enough free
resources to fulfill

00:11:54.680 --> 00:11:56.870
the requirements of the pod.

00:11:56.870 --> 00:11:58.560
We have two options.

00:11:58.560 --> 00:12:00.830
We can either free
up some resources

00:12:00.830 --> 00:12:08.020
by deleting either unneeded
or lower priority pods,

00:12:08.020 --> 00:12:12.330
or we can just add more
resources to our cluster.

00:12:12.330 --> 00:12:15.070
We live in a cloud
world, so of course there

00:12:15.070 --> 00:12:17.590
is no need to call
our technician,

00:12:17.590 --> 00:12:20.950
or, let's say,
order new servers.

00:12:20.950 --> 00:12:26.410
We can just go to Google
Cloud and request more VMs.

00:12:26.410 --> 00:12:28.990
But can we automate
this process?

00:12:28.990 --> 00:12:31.570
At Google, we love automation.

00:12:31.570 --> 00:12:34.090
So that's why we introduced
Cluster Autoscaler.

00:12:36.950 --> 00:12:39.590
The idea behind it
is fairly simple.

00:12:39.590 --> 00:12:42.280
Cluster Autoscaler
increases cluster size

00:12:42.280 --> 00:12:47.700
by adding new nodes to
be more precise here.

00:12:47.700 --> 00:12:49.750
The nodes are VMs
in Google Compute

00:12:49.750 --> 00:12:55.470
Engine to the cluster when
an unscheduled pod appears.

00:12:55.470 --> 00:12:57.690
In a similar way, when
a Cluster Autoscaler

00:12:57.690 --> 00:13:02.370
can see unneeded nodes,
it will remove them.

00:13:02.370 --> 00:13:05.410
As I mentioned, the
idea is simple, but not

00:13:05.410 --> 00:13:08.150
the implementation.

00:13:08.150 --> 00:13:13.750
For example, scaling down
is an NP-hard problem.

00:13:13.750 --> 00:13:16.730
There is also a number of
other hard technical problems

00:13:16.730 --> 00:13:17.840
to solve there.

00:13:17.840 --> 00:13:21.090
If you are interested
in this area,

00:13:21.090 --> 00:13:23.780
please join
Kubernetes community,

00:13:23.780 --> 00:13:27.740
the work around Cluster
Autoscaler is open to everyone.

00:13:30.560 --> 00:13:33.650
And the Cluster Autoscaler
is currently in beta,

00:13:33.650 --> 00:13:36.950
but we've gotten a lot of
good feedback from users

00:13:36.950 --> 00:13:39.710
and have made significant
performance improvements that

00:13:39.710 --> 00:13:45.420
will be coming with the
1.6 release of Kubernetes.

00:13:45.420 --> 00:13:49.360
Let me show you
how Scale Up works.

00:13:49.360 --> 00:13:52.150
So a new, unscheduled
[INAUDIBLE] pod

00:13:52.150 --> 00:13:53.860
appear in our cluster.

00:13:53.860 --> 00:13:57.250
Cluster Autoscaler will
perform scheduling simulation

00:13:57.250 --> 00:13:59.330
to check whether
adding a new node

00:13:59.330 --> 00:14:02.460
will help with
scheduling this pod.

00:14:02.460 --> 00:14:06.880
And in case the
answer is yes, it

00:14:06.880 --> 00:14:08.800
will send a request
to Google Cloud

00:14:08.800 --> 00:14:12.610
to create a new VM, which
will be adopted by Kubernetes

00:14:12.610 --> 00:14:15.880
and become yet another
node in the cluster.

00:14:15.880 --> 00:14:21.930
Then scheduler will retry to
place the pod to the new node.

00:14:21.930 --> 00:14:25.120
Scale down is slightly
more complicated.

00:14:25.120 --> 00:14:27.780
So let's imagine that
we have a cluster.

00:14:27.780 --> 00:14:30.840
We have some pods running there.

00:14:30.840 --> 00:14:33.100
The state of cluster
changes over time.

00:14:33.100 --> 00:14:35.500
Some pods are
created or deleted,

00:14:35.500 --> 00:14:42.150
either manually or automatically
by Horizontal Pod Autoscaler.

00:14:42.150 --> 00:14:47.190
So we can see that a pod
from Node B was deleted.

00:14:47.190 --> 00:14:52.410
And a pod from Node
A was deleted too.

00:14:52.410 --> 00:14:55.540
Yet another pod from
Node A was deleted.

00:14:55.540 --> 00:14:59.580
So we ended up in a
state where the cluster

00:14:59.580 --> 00:15:01.700
is pretty low utilized.

00:15:01.700 --> 00:15:05.730
Cluster Autoscaler observes
the cluster state all the time.

00:15:05.730 --> 00:15:09.420
It will detect such situation
and perform a simulation

00:15:09.420 --> 00:15:12.660
whether it's possible
to safely remove a node.

00:15:12.660 --> 00:15:17.400
What I mean by safely remove
is all pods from the node

00:15:17.400 --> 00:15:20.910
will find a spot
across other nodes.

00:15:20.910 --> 00:15:22.940
In this case, the answer is yes.

00:15:22.940 --> 00:15:27.690
Green pod from Node C can
be rescheduled to Node A.

00:15:27.690 --> 00:15:30.940
So the node C is empty now.

00:15:30.940 --> 00:15:32.690
So it can be safely removed.

00:15:35.360 --> 00:15:40.000
So the benefits from using
Cluster Autoscaler are clear.

00:15:40.000 --> 00:15:43.000
It allows to handle the
service popularity growth,

00:15:43.000 --> 00:15:46.480
or just a temporary traffic
spike automatically.

00:15:46.480 --> 00:15:49.740
It saves money when the
cluster is low utilized.

00:15:53.500 --> 00:15:57.280
So up until now, we've
been troubleshooting

00:15:57.280 --> 00:15:59.080
with command line.

00:15:59.080 --> 00:16:03.760
But some of us, like Steve,
an engineer on Susan's team,

00:16:03.760 --> 00:16:06.480
are wondering if
there is another way.

00:16:06.480 --> 00:16:08.260
It's 2017.

00:16:08.260 --> 00:16:10.170
We are immigrating
to Cloud world.

00:16:10.170 --> 00:16:13.390
Can we migrate old-fashioned
command line tool

00:16:13.390 --> 00:16:15.850
to a new, shiny UI?

00:16:15.850 --> 00:16:17.060
No problem.

00:16:17.060 --> 00:16:20.890
Kubernetes provides a great
dashboard, which allows you to,

00:16:20.890 --> 00:16:23.560
for example, to do all
troubleshooting that we've

00:16:23.560 --> 00:16:25.250
done so far.

00:16:25.250 --> 00:16:27.310
And don't be afraid,
there is no plan

00:16:27.310 --> 00:16:29.210
to sunset great,
old-fashioned kubectl.

00:16:32.280 --> 00:16:35.110
So we can see
Kubernetes dashboard,

00:16:35.110 --> 00:16:42.070
which offers a view of
events within the cluster.

00:16:42.070 --> 00:16:47.280
You can see logs
there and metrics,

00:16:47.280 --> 00:16:50.910
which is an area where
Kubernetes dashboard is better

00:16:50.910 --> 00:16:51.600
than kubectl.

00:16:51.600 --> 00:16:55.110
You can see some
visualization, but only

00:16:55.110 --> 00:16:57.260
from a couple of last minutes.

00:17:00.600 --> 00:17:04.589
So we've seen a lot of
useful tools so far,

00:17:04.589 --> 00:17:09.150
such as real time monitoring,
real time logging,

00:17:09.150 --> 00:17:11.940
support for basic
troubleshooting.

00:17:11.940 --> 00:17:14.220
But to have a full
blown monitoring

00:17:14.220 --> 00:17:18.329
solution, which is sufficient
for production use,

00:17:18.329 --> 00:17:19.810
we need more.

00:17:19.810 --> 00:17:24.477
Deepak can tell you
what your options are.

00:17:24.477 --> 00:17:25.810
DEEPAK TIWARI: Thank you, Piotr.

00:17:28.700 --> 00:17:32.720
So I totally agree with
Piotr on both counts.

00:17:32.720 --> 00:17:35.590
First one, totally
love GKE, provides

00:17:35.590 --> 00:17:39.250
you native, inbuilt
solution and tooling,

00:17:39.250 --> 00:17:42.670
if you wanted to look at how
your application is performing,

00:17:42.670 --> 00:17:45.022
where you're identifying an
issue when it's happening,

00:17:45.022 --> 00:17:47.230
and then the ability to set
up auto scaling for nodes

00:17:47.230 --> 00:17:48.230
and clusters.

00:17:48.230 --> 00:17:49.870
And that's very, very useful.

00:17:49.870 --> 00:17:52.390
But we also know
that when you are

00:17:52.390 --> 00:17:54.820
running in production
environment, when you're

00:17:54.820 --> 00:17:57.520
running large number
of nodes and clusters,

00:17:57.520 --> 00:18:02.830
that you actually sometimes need
more powerful tools and more

00:18:02.830 --> 00:18:04.720
capability for
monitoring, and logging,

00:18:04.720 --> 00:18:06.586
and for running your operations.

00:18:06.586 --> 00:18:08.210
So what are some of
these capabilities?

00:18:08.210 --> 00:18:11.051
These capabilities are
things like historical,

00:18:11.051 --> 00:18:12.050
monitoring, and logging.

00:18:12.050 --> 00:18:14.830
So you might want to actually
not look at the things

00:18:14.830 --> 00:18:16.360
as they're happening,
but you might

00:18:16.360 --> 00:18:18.797
want to actually look at the
data, what happened maybe

00:18:18.797 --> 00:18:19.630
this Monday morning.

00:18:19.630 --> 00:18:21.220
And you might want
to see something

00:18:21.220 --> 00:18:25.060
that happened on Friday, or
maybe something that happened,

00:18:25.060 --> 00:18:26.590
there was a spike
a week ago and you

00:18:26.590 --> 00:18:29.485
wanted to see how this week
looks compared to that.

00:18:29.485 --> 00:18:32.430
So Historical Data, time series
data, as well as logging,

00:18:32.430 --> 00:18:35.770
you want to look at that,
start to compare things.

00:18:35.770 --> 00:18:37.720
Also you want to do
advanced troubleshooting.

00:18:37.720 --> 00:18:40.230
So what you want to do is
you want to start looking at,

00:18:40.230 --> 00:18:41.980
if you're looking at
a particular handler,

00:18:41.980 --> 00:18:44.290
or a particular machine,
or particular application,

00:18:44.290 --> 00:18:47.400
you want to be able to do
some sophisticated queries

00:18:47.400 --> 00:18:52.060
so you can actually narrow
down a particular container,

00:18:52.060 --> 00:18:54.100
or particular pods,
or particular nodes

00:18:54.100 --> 00:18:55.930
that you were looking
at or application

00:18:55.930 --> 00:18:57.792
and see what was
happening there.

00:18:57.792 --> 00:18:59.250
And then of course,
visualizations,

00:18:59.250 --> 00:19:02.950
so you know great
tools available in GKE,

00:19:02.950 --> 00:19:05.860
but for more visualization
on charting, distribution

00:19:05.860 --> 00:19:08.080
metrics, and things
like that, that

00:19:08.080 --> 00:19:09.877
would be very, very useful.

00:19:09.877 --> 00:19:11.210
And finally setting up alerting.

00:19:11.210 --> 00:19:13.540
So when you are looking
at these metrics,

00:19:13.540 --> 00:19:15.700
looking at your
log data, you might

00:19:15.700 --> 00:19:19.450
want to create metric ad hoc
on that and then set up alerts

00:19:19.450 --> 00:19:24.040
so that things, errors and
exceptions, are [? pilot ?]

00:19:24.040 --> 00:19:25.810
and see whenever
those things happen,

00:19:25.810 --> 00:19:29.022
you get paged or
alerted on that.

00:19:29.022 --> 00:19:31.480
So we'll talk about what are
the solutions and options that

00:19:31.480 --> 00:19:33.520
are available.

00:19:33.520 --> 00:19:38.040
And of course, first we'll
talk about Google Stackdriver.

00:19:38.040 --> 00:19:39.290
Sp what is Google Stackdriver?

00:19:39.290 --> 00:19:42.580
So let's give a little bit
of introduction about this.

00:19:42.580 --> 00:19:46.270
Here we have a very brief
summary of what Stackdriver is.

00:19:46.270 --> 00:19:50.170
In summary, it's basically
our GCP native solution

00:19:50.170 --> 00:19:53.000
for monitoring, and
logging, and diagnostics,

00:19:53.000 --> 00:19:56.650
so you can keep running your
application without any worries

00:19:56.650 --> 00:19:59.500
and identify issues
as they're happening.

00:19:59.500 --> 00:20:02.140
But I wanted to draw your
attention to the keywords

00:20:02.140 --> 00:20:03.610
that I have here in bold.

00:20:03.610 --> 00:20:08.360
So the first one is that
it's an integrated solution.

00:20:08.360 --> 00:20:12.160
So when we started to
envision Stackdriver,

00:20:12.160 --> 00:20:15.280
we started to see that in
the marketplace, as well

00:20:15.280 --> 00:20:18.430
as we talk to customers, they
are using a logging solution.

00:20:18.430 --> 00:20:19.930
They're using a
monitoring solution.

00:20:19.930 --> 00:20:22.840
They're using some type
solution and hooking things up,

00:20:22.840 --> 00:20:25.510
but some alerting solutions.

00:20:25.510 --> 00:20:27.940
Then sometimes they
have a separate error

00:20:27.940 --> 00:20:29.710
reporting solution.

00:20:29.710 --> 00:20:33.370
And then diagnostics and other
application level monitoring,

00:20:33.370 --> 00:20:34.714
they are using something else.

00:20:34.714 --> 00:20:36.880
But we realized that really
what you're trying to do

00:20:36.880 --> 00:20:38.410
is it's a single workflow.

00:20:38.410 --> 00:20:40.540
You had an issue,
something happened.

00:20:40.540 --> 00:20:42.130
You need to figure
out what happened.

00:20:42.130 --> 00:20:44.680
Go identify the
issue and resolve it.

00:20:44.680 --> 00:20:47.980
So we envision this as
one single product which

00:20:47.980 --> 00:20:50.860
has all of these
capabilities inside it,

00:20:50.860 --> 00:20:54.920
one single go-to market,
one single package.

00:20:54.920 --> 00:20:56.170
Second one is SaaS.

00:20:56.170 --> 00:20:59.640
So as you also heard
in the keynote today,

00:20:59.640 --> 00:21:02.390
take an example of "Pokemon GO."

00:21:02.390 --> 00:21:05.810
They mentioned that
they saw traffic,

00:21:05.810 --> 00:21:09.200
which was 50 times more
than not just their average,

00:21:09.200 --> 00:21:11.540
but their best estimation.

00:21:11.540 --> 00:21:15.800
So what happens is, of
course, as Piotr mentioned,

00:21:15.800 --> 00:21:16.569
you can autoscale.

00:21:16.569 --> 00:21:18.110
And you can get more
computing power.

00:21:18.110 --> 00:21:21.140
And you can get more storage
power and all that good stuff.

00:21:21.140 --> 00:21:24.080
But also you have to realize
that you're monitoring

00:21:24.080 --> 00:21:25.880
and logging solutions
also need to scale.

00:21:25.880 --> 00:21:28.670
So now you're sending perhaps
50 times more log data,

00:21:28.670 --> 00:21:31.580
perhaps 50 times more monitoring
data than you envisioned.

00:21:31.580 --> 00:21:33.650
And if you are running
your own clusters,

00:21:33.650 --> 00:21:36.800
you have to now start to
see what you provisioned

00:21:36.800 --> 00:21:40.500
and resize your clusters
and manage that.

00:21:40.500 --> 00:21:43.200
With Google Stackdriver,
you have to do none of that.

00:21:43.200 --> 00:21:45.980
It's completely managed
for SaaS solution.

00:21:45.980 --> 00:21:48.560
And then the last one is,
it's the hybrid solution.

00:21:48.560 --> 00:21:52.760
So of course, we all live
in a multi-cloud world.

00:21:52.760 --> 00:21:54.710
And we also live
in a world where

00:21:54.710 --> 00:21:59.150
predominantly people's companies
still have loads on-prem.

00:21:59.150 --> 00:22:02.120
So there are use
cases where you would

00:22:02.120 --> 00:22:03.770
want a single pane of glass.

00:22:03.770 --> 00:22:07.700
So we are not built only for
GCP-- we have built in GCP,

00:22:07.700 --> 00:22:09.770
but we are not only for GCP.

00:22:09.770 --> 00:22:12.140
We are built for any cloud.

00:22:12.140 --> 00:22:15.750
And right now we officially
support monitoring and logging

00:22:15.750 --> 00:22:17.692
for AWS.

00:22:17.692 --> 00:22:20.090
And in the future,
we'll expand that.

00:22:20.090 --> 00:22:23.600
But right now we already provide
capabilities with public APIs

00:22:23.600 --> 00:22:25.240
that you can actually bring in.

00:22:25.240 --> 00:22:26.990
Your application might
be running anywhere

00:22:26.990 --> 00:22:29.060
and you can bring in
some of those signals

00:22:29.060 --> 00:22:32.450
from anywhere, from any
cloud, or even on-prem.

00:22:32.450 --> 00:22:34.820
So that's really sort of
the vision of Stackdriver

00:22:34.820 --> 00:22:37.110
and how we are
thinking about that.

00:22:37.110 --> 00:22:39.470
So this, again,
talks very briefly

00:22:39.470 --> 00:22:41.269
about things that
I mentioned, which

00:22:41.269 --> 00:22:42.810
is the first point
around integrated.

00:22:42.810 --> 00:22:45.080
So within the
Stackdriver package,

00:22:45.080 --> 00:22:48.290
you'll see a
Monitoring solution,

00:22:48.290 --> 00:22:51.430
Logging solution, monitoring
to bring in all the time

00:22:51.430 --> 00:22:54.560
series metric data, and then
visualizing them, setting up

00:22:54.560 --> 00:23:00.460
alerts, logging data to bring in
freeform, high volume log data.

00:23:00.460 --> 00:23:03.470
And then debugging, if you're
hosting your code on GCP,

00:23:03.470 --> 00:23:05.150
you can actually
hook that up so you

00:23:05.150 --> 00:23:07.460
can actually see when
errors are happening,

00:23:07.460 --> 00:23:08.810
exceptions are happening.

00:23:08.810 --> 00:23:11.120
You can go to the line of
the code that is actually

00:23:11.120 --> 00:23:13.560
triggering that and
you can debug it.

00:23:13.560 --> 00:23:17.660
And then Trace is where you
want to get full stack trace.

00:23:17.660 --> 00:23:20.780
You can go to Tracing
and then Error Reporting,

00:23:20.780 --> 00:23:23.660
proactively identify the
exceptions and errors happening

00:23:23.660 --> 00:23:26.570
in your application, and
gives you visibility into that

00:23:26.570 --> 00:23:28.100
without you hooking anything up.

00:23:28.100 --> 00:23:30.750
So again, great
out-of-the-box solution there.

00:23:30.750 --> 00:23:34.600
So [? sample ?] all of
that in Stackdriver, be it

00:23:34.600 --> 00:23:37.710
building, innovating,
[INAUDIBLE].

00:23:37.710 --> 00:23:40.050
In terms of different
signals, like so

00:23:40.050 --> 00:23:41.230
what is really happening?

00:23:41.230 --> 00:23:43.200
So like I mentioned,
we are building truly

00:23:43.200 --> 00:23:44.970
for a hybrid world.

00:23:44.970 --> 00:23:47.130
So you might be actually
on any cloud servers.

00:23:47.130 --> 00:23:51.100
And you can even envision
not a cloud environment,

00:23:51.100 --> 00:23:53.340
but we support that.

00:23:53.340 --> 00:23:56.790
Like I mentioned, AWS and
GCP officially supported.

00:23:56.790 --> 00:23:59.190
And then you might be
running any operating system.

00:23:59.190 --> 00:24:02.280
We support all the key
operating systems today.

00:24:02.280 --> 00:24:06.570
And then you would be running,
so your VMs and servers

00:24:06.570 --> 00:24:09.220
would be running that, and
then finally your application.

00:24:09.220 --> 00:24:12.090
So all of these are
generating a large amount

00:24:12.090 --> 00:24:15.900
of data, which come in form
of, as I was mentioning,

00:24:15.900 --> 00:24:18.270
Log, Metrics, Error
Traces and Events.

00:24:18.270 --> 00:24:21.870
And we can take all of that and
provide a single way for you

00:24:21.870 --> 00:24:24.841
to analyze that Get
Notification on that

00:24:24.841 --> 00:24:25.840
and visualize that data.

00:24:29.910 --> 00:24:33.200
So let's talk about a little
bit of detail of GKE monitoring

00:24:33.200 --> 00:24:34.380
and logging capabilities.

00:24:34.380 --> 00:24:38.390
So for example here, in very
brief, you will see monitoring,

00:24:38.390 --> 00:24:40.700
when we talk about,
again, time series data,

00:24:40.700 --> 00:24:43.580
you are used to seeing these
kind of charts and dashboards

00:24:43.580 --> 00:24:45.950
whichever solution
you use, and that's

00:24:45.950 --> 00:24:47.690
what we're talking
about in monitoring.

00:24:47.690 --> 00:24:50.420
And then logging, you
basically have all the log data

00:24:50.420 --> 00:24:53.610
that's like a time
stamp, structured

00:24:53.610 --> 00:24:55.670
with a text
[INAUDIBLE] coming in.

00:24:55.670 --> 00:24:57.770
And you might want
to do more queries

00:24:57.770 --> 00:24:59.894
on that data in
real time, and look

00:24:59.894 --> 00:25:02.060
through what is really
happening, and slice and dice

00:25:02.060 --> 00:25:02.870
that data.

00:25:02.870 --> 00:25:05.060
And then perhaps,
actually, use the log data

00:25:05.060 --> 00:25:09.290
to create more metrics that
feed onto the metric side.

00:25:09.290 --> 00:25:13.280
So that's really at a high
level what we provide for GKE.

00:25:13.280 --> 00:25:17.300
Now let's go into a
little more detail.

00:25:17.300 --> 00:25:21.620
So at a high level, the
way we think about it,

00:25:21.620 --> 00:25:24.740
or I think about
it, is divided here

00:25:24.740 --> 00:25:26.644
into four high level steps.

00:25:26.644 --> 00:25:27.560
One is Data ingestion.

00:25:27.560 --> 00:25:31.290
You really care about how do I
get my data into Stackdriver,

00:25:31.290 --> 00:25:34.160
whether I'm running
GKE or something else.

00:25:34.160 --> 00:25:36.920
And then how do I
manage that data?

00:25:36.920 --> 00:25:39.972
What kind of tools that are
available to me for management,

00:25:39.972 --> 00:25:41.430
and what hierarchies
do you support

00:25:41.430 --> 00:25:44.660
so I can define a
cluster, perhaps, and then

00:25:44.660 --> 00:25:46.730
see data that way?

00:25:46.730 --> 00:25:49.381
And then what's happening
with visualization?

00:25:49.381 --> 00:25:51.380
What are the capabilities
available to me there?

00:25:51.380 --> 00:25:52.820
How to actually see this?

00:25:52.820 --> 00:25:55.340
And then finally, this
is really important

00:25:55.340 --> 00:25:58.250
because we are building
GCP in a very open way.

00:25:58.250 --> 00:26:00.486
Similarly, we're building
GKE in a very open way.

00:26:00.486 --> 00:26:03.110
And then we are building Google
Stackdriver in a very open way.

00:26:03.110 --> 00:26:06.200
So it's not just about
providing the product,

00:26:06.200 --> 00:26:08.640
but also providing
you the data service.

00:26:08.640 --> 00:26:11.470
If you did not want to
use Google Stackdriver,

00:26:11.470 --> 00:26:13.160
but want to take all
of that data out,

00:26:13.160 --> 00:26:15.390
you have that
capability as well.

00:26:15.390 --> 00:26:19.400
So let's talk about that first
piece here, data ingestion.

00:26:19.400 --> 00:26:24.920
So today, any log that you
have-- system log, audit log,

00:26:24.920 --> 00:26:26.390
or application log--

00:26:26.390 --> 00:26:29.720
would come in by default
into Stackdriver from GKE

00:26:29.720 --> 00:26:32.090
through stderr and stdout.

00:26:32.090 --> 00:26:34.034
Now that's not
the best solution.

00:26:34.034 --> 00:26:35.450
So I'll talk to
you about Road Map

00:26:35.450 --> 00:26:37.860
and how we are going to
enhance that solution.

00:26:37.860 --> 00:26:40.130
But today, that part
is available for you

00:26:40.130 --> 00:26:44.370
to bring data into
Stackdriver by default.

00:26:44.370 --> 00:26:48.020
The other thing that
you're interested in

00:26:48.020 --> 00:26:48.860
is in metric side.

00:26:48.860 --> 00:26:51.410
And so Heapster and
cadvisor already

00:26:51.410 --> 00:26:53.240
send system level
metrics, again,

00:26:53.240 --> 00:26:54.650
by default, to Stackdriver.

00:26:54.650 --> 00:26:57.990
So you would be able to
see that at the node level.

00:26:57.990 --> 00:26:59.930
Then we have public
APIs available,

00:26:59.930 --> 00:27:02.670
both for custom metrics
as well as full logs.

00:27:02.670 --> 00:27:05.510
So you can write any metric
from your application,

00:27:05.510 --> 00:27:08.080
use our client libraries,
built for the metrics, again,

00:27:08.080 --> 00:27:12.680
and for logs, or send any
custom log that you might have.

00:27:12.680 --> 00:27:15.740
So that's really the capability
that's available for GKE,

00:27:15.740 --> 00:27:18.190
in terms of what you can
bring in to Stackdriver

00:27:18.190 --> 00:27:19.930
and how you can bring that in.

00:27:19.930 --> 00:27:23.840
Now, as I mentioned, we'll touch
upon little bit of the roadmap

00:27:23.840 --> 00:27:25.179
and what's coming.

00:27:25.179 --> 00:27:27.470
But what you will see there
is what we're missing today

00:27:27.470 --> 00:27:29.650
is application level metric.

00:27:29.650 --> 00:27:34.114
So it's not very easy today to
have a package solution that

00:27:34.114 --> 00:27:35.030
brings all of that in.

00:27:35.030 --> 00:27:36.920
So we'll talk about that.

00:27:36.920 --> 00:27:39.440
But let's talk about sort
of management and hierarchy.

00:27:39.440 --> 00:27:41.000
So the way Stackdriver
is set up is

00:27:41.000 --> 00:27:44.060
that you have a concept
of Stackdriver accounts,

00:27:44.060 --> 00:27:46.352
and you can keep multiple
projects that you

00:27:46.352 --> 00:27:48.560
might be running on the
cloud within that Stackdriver

00:27:48.560 --> 00:27:49.490
account.

00:27:49.490 --> 00:27:53.060
And you can see the metrics
from those multiple projects

00:27:53.060 --> 00:27:53.790
in a single view.

00:27:53.790 --> 00:27:55.165
So you can create
a single chart,

00:27:55.165 --> 00:27:56.960
for example, or a
single dashboard,

00:27:56.960 --> 00:28:01.340
where you're seeing signals
from all of that in one place.

00:28:01.340 --> 00:28:02.860
So you might have
multiple clusters

00:28:02.860 --> 00:28:04.620
that you might be running,
some maybe one running front,

00:28:04.620 --> 00:28:06.920
one running back end
in different projects,

00:28:06.920 --> 00:28:09.710
but you can visualize
them together.

00:28:09.710 --> 00:28:11.100
So that's really useful.

00:28:11.100 --> 00:28:13.680
And again, within the projects,
you have concept of groups.

00:28:13.680 --> 00:28:17.870
So within a project, you might
be running multiple clusters.

00:28:17.870 --> 00:28:21.810
But if you want to define
a group based off of nodes,

00:28:21.810 --> 00:28:23.370
you can do that.

00:28:23.370 --> 00:28:25.820
And you can, for example,
again, some stuff

00:28:25.820 --> 00:28:29.360
is running maybe on
staging versus broad.

00:28:29.360 --> 00:28:32.630
And you can identify that
and create groups, and then

00:28:32.630 --> 00:28:35.310
see the metrics
only at that level.

00:28:35.310 --> 00:28:37.470
So you have that capability.

00:28:37.470 --> 00:28:42.890
Charts and dashboard,
so you can, again,

00:28:42.890 --> 00:28:46.680
visualize all this data, both
the log data that's coming in,

00:28:46.680 --> 00:28:48.660
and then create
log based metrics,

00:28:48.660 --> 00:28:51.150
visualize that, as well
as the system metrics

00:28:51.150 --> 00:28:54.060
that we talked about is
coming in, you can visualize

00:28:54.060 --> 00:28:56.399
or any custom metrics
that you're bringing in.

00:28:56.399 --> 00:28:57.940
You can create your
custom dashboard.

00:28:57.940 --> 00:29:01.050
And then you can set
up alerting on that.

00:29:01.050 --> 00:29:04.410
And then data liberation,
touching upon that quickly,

00:29:04.410 --> 00:29:07.050
you have the read APIs
both for monitoring

00:29:07.050 --> 00:29:08.050
as well as for logging.

00:29:08.050 --> 00:29:11.550
So you have metric data
as well as log data

00:29:11.550 --> 00:29:13.950
that you can use read APIs
or take that data out.

00:29:13.950 --> 00:29:16.994
But you also have for logs,
because we know that logging

00:29:16.994 --> 00:29:19.410
data sometimes, when you're
taking it and transferring it,

00:29:19.410 --> 00:29:22.660
you need a more robust
pipeline to be able to do that.

00:29:22.660 --> 00:29:28.080
And we provide logs export to
BigQuery, GCS, and Pub/Sub,

00:29:28.080 --> 00:29:30.045
again, at no charge.

00:29:30.045 --> 00:29:33.660
This is free of charge for
all the GKE log data that's

00:29:33.660 --> 00:29:40.200
coming in, so which also gives
you the power of other GCP

00:29:40.200 --> 00:29:42.870
products, like BigQuery, so
you can take all your log data

00:29:42.870 --> 00:29:45.870
there and you can do, perhaps,
more analysis on your log data

00:29:45.870 --> 00:29:47.090
there.

00:29:47.090 --> 00:29:49.680
We store the log data
by default for 30 days,

00:29:49.680 --> 00:29:53.490
but you could perhaps even
keep it for longer in BigQuery,

00:29:53.490 --> 00:29:56.370
or you can take it
for archival into GCS.

00:29:56.370 --> 00:29:59.490
And then we also
send log data, again,

00:29:59.490 --> 00:30:01.890
pretty much in real
time to Pub/Sub.

00:30:01.890 --> 00:30:03.750
So you can actually
stream the data out

00:30:03.750 --> 00:30:06.420
and you can take that to
a third party solution.

00:30:06.420 --> 00:30:11.340
We have partnership with
many commercial solutions,

00:30:11.340 --> 00:30:12.480
like Splunk.

00:30:12.480 --> 00:30:15.360
And you can take the data,
for example, if you wanted to,

00:30:15.360 --> 00:30:19.920
all your GKE logs or
any of the logs there.

00:30:19.920 --> 00:30:21.860
So what's really coming
up on the roadmap?

00:30:21.860 --> 00:30:24.120
So I think the two
things that I think

00:30:24.120 --> 00:30:26.690
I wanted to say one
thing very emphatically

00:30:26.690 --> 00:30:29.960
is that we are fully
committed to provide

00:30:29.960 --> 00:30:33.530
the best in class solution for
GKE monitoring and logging.

00:30:33.530 --> 00:30:35.570
What's missing that
is application level

00:30:35.570 --> 00:30:37.670
monitoring from that.

00:30:37.670 --> 00:30:39.620
And we are working
very hard on that.

00:30:39.620 --> 00:30:42.470
We are working on as
meta data solution

00:30:42.470 --> 00:30:45.080
to be able to capture
meta data agent,

00:30:45.080 --> 00:30:47.450
to be able to capture
all the information,

00:30:47.450 --> 00:30:49.640
as well as provide you
with application level

00:30:49.640 --> 00:30:54.740
monitoring, as well as logging
in a more agent-based fashion,

00:30:54.740 --> 00:30:57.410
rather than taking it
from stderr or stdout,

00:30:57.410 --> 00:31:01.820
or from just relying on
Heapster and cadvisor.

00:31:01.820 --> 00:31:05.394
So there will be an
agent-based solution for both.

00:31:05.394 --> 00:31:06.560
And then you've stalled out.

00:31:06.560 --> 00:31:09.170
And you do not worry about
anything else, application

00:31:09.170 --> 00:31:12.162
system, audit level information
is all available to you.

00:31:12.162 --> 00:31:14.120
And then the second thing
that we're working on

00:31:14.120 --> 00:31:16.860
is a curator dashboard for GKE.

00:31:16.860 --> 00:31:19.400
So if you're running GKE
cluster or GKE nodes,

00:31:19.400 --> 00:31:23.420
then you would be able
to see, in Stackdriver,

00:31:23.420 --> 00:31:24.765
a fully curated dashboard.

00:31:24.765 --> 00:31:28.250
So those are the two things we
are working on, in that order.

00:31:28.250 --> 00:31:31.150
And you will see both of
those later part of this year.

00:31:33.710 --> 00:31:38.020
So with that said, this morning
in keynote, Eric Schmidt

00:31:38.020 --> 00:31:39.970
talked about sort of
freedom to innovate

00:31:39.970 --> 00:31:41.290
but also freedom to choose.

00:31:41.290 --> 00:31:43.210
So again, we're building
it in a way where

00:31:43.210 --> 00:31:44.980
you are not restricted to that.

00:31:44.980 --> 00:31:47.560
If you wanted to choose
a third party solution,

00:31:47.560 --> 00:31:50.590
there are many good open source
solution, commercial solutions

00:31:50.590 --> 00:31:52.370
available out there.

00:31:52.370 --> 00:31:53.920
Here are a few
that we have worked

00:31:53.920 --> 00:31:56.440
read that we have heard
that customers like.

00:31:56.440 --> 00:31:59.890
And so one is Prometheus.

00:31:59.890 --> 00:32:02.890
It's an open source
toolkit, as you're aware,

00:32:02.890 --> 00:32:05.370
that gets you application
level metrics.

00:32:05.370 --> 00:32:08.440
It scrapes all of that from GKE.

00:32:08.440 --> 00:32:12.340
And it's an open source
solution and provides you

00:32:12.340 --> 00:32:15.840
some light level of dashboarding
as well, like PromDash

00:32:15.840 --> 00:32:19.250
and things like that,
so a very good solution.

00:32:19.250 --> 00:32:22.000
Similarly, Sysdig, third-party
monitoring solution,

00:32:22.000 --> 00:32:24.730
they are built
natively for container.

00:32:24.730 --> 00:32:25.900
That's how they came out.

00:32:25.900 --> 00:32:28.025
They were built for container
monitoring, so again,

00:32:28.025 --> 00:32:31.370
a good solution, a
commercial solution.

00:32:31.370 --> 00:32:35.380
And Datadog, Datadog is a more
generic, third-party monitoring

00:32:35.380 --> 00:32:36.320
solution.

00:32:36.320 --> 00:32:38.695
But they have really up their
game on the container world

00:32:38.695 --> 00:32:41.170
as well, so again, a good
solution and a good option.

00:32:41.170 --> 00:32:45.050
And then for log data, Splunk,
again, like I was mentioning,

00:32:45.050 --> 00:32:49.230
we have a Pub/Sub
API-based integration

00:32:49.230 --> 00:32:50.480
available already with Splunk.

00:32:50.480 --> 00:32:52.300
So again, through one
click, if you already

00:32:52.300 --> 00:32:55.440
have a Splunk running in
your internal cluster,

00:32:55.440 --> 00:32:58.960
or Splunk cloud, you're running
that, one click contribution,

00:32:58.960 --> 00:33:02.440
you can send all your log
data to a Pub/Sub topic

00:33:02.440 --> 00:33:05.200
and then Splunk can
subscribe it to your account,

00:33:05.200 --> 00:33:07.180
can subscribed to it.

00:33:07.180 --> 00:33:10.000
We have a well-documented and
validated solution on that.

00:33:10.000 --> 00:33:12.910
So those are the
things that we are

00:33:12.910 --> 00:33:17.470
excited about offering
for GKE and recommending,

00:33:17.470 --> 00:33:21.460
as well as what is
available within Stackdriver

00:33:21.460 --> 00:33:24.070
for you to look at, and
what we're building.

00:33:24.070 --> 00:33:25.900
We're really, really
excited about that.

00:33:25.900 --> 00:33:29.590
And I'll give it back to Piotr.

00:33:29.590 --> 00:33:32.350
PIOTR SZCZESNIAK: So basically
coming back to our team,

00:33:32.350 --> 00:33:35.680
they are happy
because they can focus

00:33:35.680 --> 00:33:41.350
on building great product,
rather than being involved

00:33:41.350 --> 00:33:46.660
in all those depths of stuff.

00:33:46.660 --> 00:33:52.130
Kubernetes and GKE offers them
built-in simple monitoring

00:33:52.130 --> 00:33:54.350
and logging solution.

00:33:54.350 --> 00:33:58.450
It offers tools for
troubleshooting.

00:33:58.450 --> 00:34:02.000
It offers various kinds of
autoscaling, both cluster

00:34:02.000 --> 00:34:08.360
and pod level, and more features
around autoscaling coming soon.

00:34:08.360 --> 00:34:12.449
GKE offers out-of-the-box
advanced instrumentation with

00:34:12.449 --> 00:34:13.760
Stackdriver.

00:34:13.760 --> 00:34:19.580
And of course, you can use
any other monitoring solution.

00:34:19.580 --> 00:34:24.710
It is possible to integrate
with other monitoring solution.

00:34:24.710 --> 00:34:27.860
I would like to thank
you very much for coming,

00:34:27.860 --> 00:34:31.070
for attending this session.

00:34:31.070 --> 00:34:35.449
If you are interested more
in Google Container Engine,

00:34:35.449 --> 00:34:40.780
I can recommend you the
following sessions, especially

00:34:40.780 --> 00:34:47.159
before the first one where
there is customers invited

00:34:47.159 --> 00:34:49.650
Philips Hue, who
will show how they

00:34:49.650 --> 00:34:55.409
use autoscaling in their
production environment.

00:34:55.409 --> 00:34:57.210
Thank you so much.

00:34:57.210 --> 00:34:59.310
[APPLAUSE]

00:34:59.310 --> 00:34:59.910
Thank you.

00:35:03.210 --> 00:35:07.160
[MUSIC PLAYING]

